{
  "dom_id": "step-q124584",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}=$",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=10{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1}=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{10{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}}{10{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1}}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
